# Lenovo IdeaPad battery conservation mode — make it writable without root.
#
# ideapad-laptop exposes the EC's ~60% charge cap as a plain sysfs
# attribute, owned root:root 0644. Reading it needs nothing; WRITING it
# needs root, which is why the Balise panel's "Charge 60%" toggle would
# otherwise be a read-only indicator. This hands write access to the
# `wheel` group once, at device-add time, so nothing has to escalate
# privileges at runtime — no pkexec, no password prompt per toggle, no
# setuid helper.
#
# Why RUN+= and a shell rather than udev's own GROUP=/MODE=: those two
# apply to a device NODE in /dev, and this is a sysfs attribute of a
# platform device, which has no node. Changing an attribute's mode is
# only reachable by actually running chgrp/chmod on it. This is the same
# shape the ThinkPad charge-threshold and ASUS fan-curve rules out in the
# wild use, for the same reason.
#
# DRIVER== rather than KERNEL=="VPC2004:00": the ACPI id varies across
# IdeaPad models, the driver that binds them does not. The [ -e ] guard
# covers the models where that driver binds but exposes no
# conservation_mode, so the rule is a no-op instead of an error there.
#
# "bind" is in the ACTION list, and it is the one that actually matters.
# DRIVER== is matched against the device's driver AT THE TIME THE EVENT IS
# PROCESSED, and the kernel registers a platform device before binding a
# driver to it: on the "add" event this device has no driver yet, so
# DRIVER== is empty and an add|change-only rule never matches. The "bind"
# event is the one emitted after ideapad_acpi has attached, and it is the
# first moment both DRIVER== and the conservation_mode attribute exist.
# "add|change" are kept so a coldplug replay (`udevadm trigger`, which
# defaults to --action=change on already-bound devices) still applies it.
#
# Installed by scripts/install-hardware.sh (the `./install hardware`
# phase), which also applies the same chgrp/chmod immediately so the
# toggle works without waiting for a reboot -- which, until that script's
# own attribute search was fixed to look under /sys/bus/platform/devices,
# it never got as far as doing on a real IdeaPad.
ACTION=="add|change|bind", SUBSYSTEM=="platform", DRIVER=="ideapad_acpi", \
  RUN+="/bin/sh -c '[ -e /sys%p/conservation_mode ] && chgrp wheel /sys%p/conservation_mode && chmod 0664 /sys%p/conservation_mode'"
